  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-IP24-SWZ151</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">More people have access to better managed and more equitable social security.</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">While Eswatini has taken significant strides to expand social protection, social protection coverage remains limited particularly for individuals in the informal sector, with only 29% of the population covered in at least one area of social protection and just 44% protected against work-related injuries. Unemployment benefits are not universally accessible. As Government and the social partners engage, addressing the fiscal space for a sustainable social protection system becomes pertinent. Efforts have been towards strengthening the collection and management of social protection statistical data through the updating of the social security inquiry. However, there is still need for an integrated national social protection statistical system. The high levels of poverty in the country have triggered tripartite engagement for the establishment of a national minimum wage.  
Building on work started in the previous biennium, the Strategy will focus on policy and capacity support towards the determination of the most appropriate governance and institutional arrangement for a Unemployment Benefit Scheme (UBS) and finalization of the UBS bill; training support to social security practitioners on managing occupational accidents and diseases and promotion of the prevention approach on occupational safety and health based on International Standards and best practices; tripartite dialogue on exploring the fiscus space for a sustainable social protection system; technical and capacity support to the tripartite partners towards establishing national institutional arrangements for national minimum wage setting; training support towards the establishment of an integrated national social protection statistical system.</narrative>
  </description>
